swbrd-ext-puddle
================

An extension is any process listening on a Unix socket that the daemon's
directory names.  The daemon dials it once per datachannel, sends a control
frame naming the peer, and then relays; the connection's lifetime is the
channel's.

This one answers the `rainboots.v1` protocol, which
/etc/swbrd/directory.d/20-puddle.toml maps here.  It is the back end of
Rainboots: a roster, a room, nicks, and an ICE candidate relay.

Identity
--------

Every channel arrives with the peer's certificate fingerprint, taken from the
finished DTLS handshake.  That is the only identity the puddle has, and the only
one it trusts.  Nicks are handed out by this process and are worth exactly what
that implies -- the client shows the peer id behind every nick for that reason.

A relayed ICE candidate carries the sender's peer id, and is dropped unless it
matches the fingerprint on the channel it arrived on.  Without that check this
would be a tool for introducing people to somebody other than who they asked
for.

State
-----

/var/lib/swbrd/puddle/nicks.json holds registered nicks and PBKDF2 hashes of
their passwords, and is the only thing this extension keeps.  Nothing said in
the room is written down or even remembered: a peer who joins late has missed
what they missed, as on IRC.

Purging the package deletes the registry; removing it does not.
